# Env vars for the Outlet app's local dev server. Copy this file to
# .env.local (preferred — see "which file" note at the bottom) and fill in
# values for the mode you're running. Never commit the filled-in file
# (.env and .env.* are gitignored; this .example file is the sole
# exception).
#
# Two supported local-dev combinations — pick ONE:
#
#   A) Real Firebase project (closest to prod, needs GCP access)
#      Set VITE_FIREBASE_* below from the Firebase console for this
#      project. Leave VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST unset/blank.
#
#   B) Firebase Auth emulator (no real GCP project needed — the default
#      for a fresh local checkout; see apps/outlet/README.md)
#      Set VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST, and set VITE_FIREBASE_* to
#      harmless dummy values (see the comment above that block for exactly
#      why dummy values work and what's actually required).
#
# If neither is set, the app boots into a clearly-labeled "auth not
# configured" state instead of crashing or silently pretending to be
# signed in — see isFirebaseConfigured in src/auth/firebase.js.

# --- Dev-server -> backend API proxy -----------------------------------
# packages/api-client/_base.js hardcodes API_BASE = "/api/v1" — a
# relative, same-origin path (a deliberate Nana/Security sign-off: it's
# what guarantees a bearer token can never be sent to a third-party
# origin, see vite.config.js's proxy comment for the full reasoning). In
# dev, Vite's dev-server proxy is what actually gets that same-origin
# request to the real backend process — see vite.config.js.
#
# Deliberately NOT VITE_-prefixed: this is read by vite.config.js itself
# (Node, via loadEnv), never by client code, so it must never be exposed
# through import.meta.env — a VITE_ prefix would do exactly that for no
# benefit.
#
# Default (if unset) is http://localhost:8000, matching where Efua
# documents running the API locally (apps/api). Only set this if you're
# running the API on a different port/host.
# DEV_API_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:8000

# --- Firebase web auth config (apps/outlet/src/auth/firebase.js) -------
# Combination A (real project): fill in real values from the Firebase
# console. Combination B (emulator, see below): any non-blank values work
# here — the SDK's own emulator flow doesn't validate them against a real
# Google Cloud project. VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN specifically is never
# even sent anywhere by this app's auth calls (verified against the SDK
# source: it's only required for popup/redirect sign-in, which this app
# never uses — see firebase.js's AUTH EMULATOR comment) — "localhost" or
# any placeholder is fine. A conventional emulator-mode placeholder set:
#   VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=demo-api-key
#   VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=localhost
#   VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=demo-ultimate-bookkeeping
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# --- Firebase Auth emulator (combination B) -----------------------------
# Set to enable local sign-in against `firebase emulators:start` instead
# of a real Firebase project — no real GCP project needed. Bare
# "host:port", NOT a URL (no http://) — this deliberately mirrors the
# shape of the backend's own FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST (the Firebase
# Admin SDK's standard env var, which Efua wires up on the API side) so
# you set the same literal value on both sides. firebase.js prepends
# "http://" itself before calling connectAuthEmulator (see that file) —
# the browser SDK needs a full URL, the env var doesn't.
#
# Only takes effect when set — leave blank/unset for combination A (real
# Firebase project). Never set this in a production build.
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099

# --- Which file: .env vs .env.local -------------------------------------
# Prefer .env.local: Vite loads .env.local with higher precedence than
# .env and (per this repo's .gitignore) .env.local is never accidentally
# committable even if someone edits .gitignore carelessly later — .env.*
# as a whole is already ignored, .env.local doubly so by convention.
